.TH SIEFS 5
.SH NAME
siefs \- the SieFS on-disk format
.SH DESCRIPTION
SieFS keeps files so that they survive anything: a crash or power cut at any
moment leaves either the state of the last commit or the one before it,
never a mix, and every block read is checked against a checksum.
Five ideas: never overwrite (a change goes to free space); one B+tree holds
everything (inodes, directory entries, extents, small files, attributes:
items sorted by a key); a commit flips one block (the superblock); parents
hold their children's checksums; small files (up to 1536 bytes) live inside
the tree.
.SS Layout
Blocks are 4 KiB, numbers little-endian.
.nf
block 0         unused (room for a boot record)
block 1, 2      the superblock, slots 0 and 1: commit s writes slot s % 2
idx[0], bm[0]   bitmap copy 0: its index (a CRC per bitmap block), 1 bit per block
idx[1], bm[1]   the same, copy 1
data_start ...  tree nodes and file data, anywhere
.fi
A bitmap block covers 32768 blocks (128 MiB); an index block holds 1024
checksums.
.SS The superblock
magic "SIEFSv1\e0", version 1, block size 4096, the volume's size, the commit
number
.B seq
(its slot is seq % 2), a block pointer to the root tree, the layout, used
blocks, creation time, UUID, label, the CRC of each index block of this
slot's bitmap copy, and a CRC-32C of the whole block.
Mounting takes the valid slot with the highest seq; if anything in it fails,
the other one: the previous commit, intact.
.SS Block pointers
40 bytes: block (8), gen (8: the commit that wrote it, checked on read),
csum (4: the block's CRC-32C), flags (4), mac (16: an encrypted block's tag).
.SS Tree nodes
One block: a 32-byte header ("NODE", level, item count, data start, commit,
tree); a leaf holds items (key 16 bytes, value offset and length) growing
up and values (at most 1900 bytes) growing down; an internal node holds
entries (key, block pointer), 72 per node.
.SS Keys and items
A key is (object id, type << 56 | offset).
Types:
.B INODE
1 (mode, uid, gid, links, size, parent, flags, times in ns),
.B INLINE
2 (a small file's contents or a symlink's target),
.B EXTENT
3 (at a byte offset: first block, up to 32 blocks, a CRC per block),
.B DIRENT
4 (by the name's hash: object, type, name),
.B XATTR
5 (attributes),
.B XINDEX
6 (the attribute index), and in the root tree
.B VOLUME
16,
.B VOLNAME
17 (a snapshot's name),
.B DEAD
18 (blocks a snapshot still uses),
.B VOLKEY
19 (an encrypted volume's sealed key).
The root directory is object 2; ids are never reused.
.SS Volumes, snapshots, encryption
The superblock points at a root tree whose items are volumes, each with its
own file tree; volume 1 is "/", others (a user's home) are mounted at a
directory entry.
An object's number is volume << 48 | id.
A snapshot is a read-only volume record sharing its head's tree at one
commit; each volume's root shows them in a hidden
.B .snapshots
directory.
An encrypted volume seals every tree node (but its header) and data block
with ChaCha20-Poly1305 under a 32-byte volume key, itself sealed in VOLKEY
by a key-encryption key derived from the owner's password (Argon2id).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B build/host/
the host tools:
.B mkfs.siefs
(make a volume),
.B fsck.siefs
(check it),
.B siefs
(read and write it),
.B siefs-dump
(show its superblocks and nodes)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fs (4),
.BR mkfs.siefs (8),
.BR snap (1),
docs/siefs.md
.SH BUGS
Files up to 2^56 bytes, volumes up to 113 TiB, names up to 255 bytes,
attribute values up to 1024 bytes.
No quotas; access times are not updated; directory listings are in hash
order.
